Wait: `restorePendingScroll` is defined after `applyBatch`, but function declarations hoist. Fine. `pendingScrollRestore` const initialized at module load before functions called. Fine.

But a subtle bug: `restorePendingScroll` returns true even when it only found an element with zero max extent and set scrollTop to 0; then `refreshLazyWindows(force)` sends range 0. Next batch, it retries. That's okay but may send spurious 0. Better: only set `restored = true` if we actually set a nonzero offset or the extent settled. But the `restored` flag triggers a force report; if nothing applied, we don't want the report. Let's refine: only count restored when `position.top > 0` or `position.left > 0` and we applied something (even clamped 0?). Hmm. If maxTop is 0, we set 0, which is not useful. Only apply/settle when extent is enough. Let's change:

```
const topSettled = position.top === 0 || maxTop >= position.top;
const leftSettled = position.left === 0 || maxLeft >= position.left;
if (topSettled && leftSettled) {
  if (position.top>0) {el.scrollTop=position.top;}
  ...
  pending.delete(id); restored = true;
}
```
This defers until the extent is large enough. But for a normal scroll container whose content is exactly the saved max and not yet padded, maxTop could equal position top eventually. Good. What if the saved offset exceeds the new content's max (list total changed)? Then it would never settle and retry forever. Use a tolerance: if maxTop > 0 and we set clamped value, consider settled when maxTop >= position.top OR maxTop >= position.top - some epsilon? Better: settle when the element's content is non-empty (maxTop>0) at all; otherwise the tree isn't materialized. So:
```
const canPlace = (position.top === 0 || maxTop > 0) && (position.left === 0 || maxLeft > 0)
```
But for a scroller with top > 0 and maxTop smaller due to fewer items, we'd settle with clamped; that's acceptable (best-effort). For a container with horizontal only, top may be 0; fine. If content not yet built, maxTop=0 and position.top>0 -> defer. Good.
```
const materialized = (position.top === 0 || maxTop > 0) && (position.left === 0 || maxLeft > 0);
if (!materialized) continue;
el.scrollTop = Math.min(...); ...
pending.delete(id); restored = true;
```
But what if the snapshot's first batch creates the scroller but the lazy rows haven't been added yet, yet the scroller has other content giving maxTop>0? It would clamp low and delete. Edge; acceptable. We could wait one more batch by requiring `el.scrollHeight >= position.top + el.clientHeight` OR the element has children. Let's use materialized as above; adequate.

Also we should only consider elements that are actually scrollable; but captured only when scrollLeft/Top>0 so they are scrollable.

Let me update the function.

